When students use AI tools to study, they passively consume answers instead of
understanding and engaging with the content. Bastani et al. (2024) found that
students with ChatGPT access improved performance by 48%. However, once the AI
was removed, grades were reduced by 17% which was worse than the performance by
students who never had access to ChatGPT. The core issue is the loss of active
effort. Students stop thinking through problems and learn passively, resulting
in the material never actually being internalized.

Lam & Tong (2024) found that points were the most effective gamification
component for engagement, since points provide immediate and visible feedback on
students' efforts that encourages continued participation. In a randomized
controlled trial, Coelho et al. (2025) found that points alone increased task
completion and enjoyment but did not improve final learning performance. However,
a combination of points, badges, and challenges produced significantly higher
learning outcomes than any single element or the control group, suggesting that
points work best alongside other gamification elements. Hernandez-Prados et al.
(2024) found that points increase intrinsic motivation by making learning more
attractive, while challenges increase engagement by setting goals and showing
students' progress. However, an improper balance of educational and gamification
elements, such as systems rewarding speed rather than accuracy and comprehension,
can divert attention from learning the material with a solid understanding.

Socrato's XP system rewards students with points for actively engaging with
study material. Students earn 25 XP for every correct quiz answer with a 15 XP
perfect-score bonus, and 10 XP for completing a flashcard session. Socrato also
tracks daily streaks, awards badges at milestones, and calculates a level from
total XP. Certain XP sources are limited to once per day to prevent spamming
activities for the purpose of gaining points rather than putting in an active
effort to learn.

Socrato's XP formula (25 XP x correct answers + 15 XP perfect bonus) rewards
accuracy, not just participation. Students cannot earn XP without actually
recalling answers. Following Coelho et al.'s findings, Socrato combines points
with streaks, badges, and levels rather than relying on any single element
alone. In addition, the daily XP cap and streaks reward continued engagement over time
instead of cramming in one session, addressing Hernandez-Prados et al.'s warning
about shallow engagement.

AI tools make it easy for students to skip the cognitive work that produces real
learning. The XP system and badges counter this by making that work feel
rewarding. When a student sees XP increase or a new badge, Socrato reinforces
active engagement rather than the passive learning resulting from the use of AI
tools. Research shows that points are one of the strongest gamification elements
for engagement, and combining them with streaks, badges, and levels keeps
students motivated over time. The XP system is a core element that turns
studying from a passive task by asking AI, into an active process students want
to carry out themselves.


